                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                        "entry_id": "63e850d1-c1ad-35bf-81f1-507f2ba8dda5",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To call or vocalize."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "intransitive",
                            "uncommon"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6c91f04e-72c3-4a03-bbbd-6a035c65ce72",
                                "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                                "text": "The Chaffinches ‘‘spinked” as though its presence was not required, but it seemed to be feeding on insects only."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e074041c-8af1-461b-8a9d-79e40fdcc613",
                                "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                                "text": "The Linnet called “ Chivvey ” can tweet and spink and song-sing, and is full of clicks and jars, like the other."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2f89c0c7-441a-43bf-8380-62b050a9663c",
                                "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                                "text": "Woodpig was rootling for worms up the brook when he was surprised to see Chaffinch, \"spinking\" at him from a hazel twig."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "80fa463c-5e84-41d0-9bb8-292384f6280e",
                                "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                                "text": "And his voice sounded good, too, and loud and clear, and he put two of his fingers into his mouth and whistled, a piercing, high-pitched scream of a whistle, and there was a sudden silence as though the noise had penetrated the brain of all things and stunned movement; but in a second the spinking blackbird began again his yittering, and all were at song again."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9660a13b-bf79-42be-8845-64281d1cac73",
                                "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                                "text": "The cry of this species, which consists of a number of rapidly ‘‘spinking” notes, reminds me of that of the Bald Eagle (Haliaeetus leucocephalus) in the United States."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "72307fdf-6742-490b-ac9c-76d046aaef76",
                                "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                                "text": "6 DECEMBER The winter sun is a flat blade of white on the horizon. Blackbirds are spinking, a charmless sound, done by rote."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "15d201fe-df06-4230-8aac-93f5715fa7dd",
                                "sense_id": "6c427079-1ae8-331e-add7-cb4e385f0a37",
                                "text": "Somewhere in the five gardens, a blackbird spinked, petulantly."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
